Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 12:45:24 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 68/71] =?UTF-8?q?inline-3d(Phase=203):=20D'=20=E2=80=94=20?=
 =?UTF-8?q?keep=20frosted=20glass=20crisp=20over=20a=20woven=20tile?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phase 3 Stage 3, browser#88. Builds entirely on Stage 2's plumbing: three
changes, no new channel.

THE PROBLEM. An excluded render pass carrying a BACKDROP FILTER over an
inline-3D tile is the worst cell of the occlusion matrix, and until now it
simply LOST. It cannot join the Phase-2 over-plane, because a backdrop
filter samples a backdrop that plane does not have. It cannot stay under
the weave, because the woven tile is drawn back opaquely over the page. So
a frosted card over a woven element disappeared behind it.

D' IS THE HONEST COMPROMISE, and it is one decision with three coupled
effects, all driven from a single recording in the split pass:

  1. the woven draw-back is CLIPPED OUT of the element's rect
     (SkClipOp::kDifference), so |output|'s own pixels — the crisp frosted
     glass the page raster already drew — show through;
  2. the same rect JOINS Stage 2's flat list, so a capable panel switches
     its physical 3D element off there and the region does not ghost
     either;
  3. what is LOST is depth behind the element. The page drew that blur
     against the FLAT page backdrop, because at page-draw time the woven
     pixels did not exist. So the scene reads flat under the glass.

That is strictly better than both alternatives — element invisible, or
element smeared through the interlace lattice — and it is why the wish's
usual "never flatten working 3D" guard does not apply to these rects: (1)
removed the woven 3D from under them, so there is nothing left to flatten.
(1) and (2) must therefore never be separated: a frosted rect in the flat
list that was NOT punched out of the draw-back would switch the lens off
over live woven pixels. Both come from the same draw-time pass, so they are
atomic by construction (risk guard R12), and they travel together on the
same GPU task all the way to the draw-back.

|own_rect|, NOT the expanded rect. Candidate now carries the quad's own
mapped+clipped rect alongside the influence rect the split already used
(they differ only for an RPDQ, whose target_rect is expanded by
GetExpandedRectForPixelMovingFilters). The frosted clip uses own_rect:
punching out a blur's REACH would leave a halo where the weave is gone and
the page has nothing to show, which is worse than the problem being fixed.

The frosted rects are NOT capped. The wire cap bounds the wish only, and
dropping a clip would resurrect the vanishing-element bug; they are counted
in the flat list's raw/sent/dropped accounting, and the throttled
plane-split marker gains frosted=N. A frosted rect that loses the flat cap
keeps its crisp pixels and merely stays 3D. Duplicate rects are harmless to
a difference clip, so the producer does not deduplicate; the draw stays ONE
whole-window blit under one save/restore, because the complement of a rect
list is not a rect and building it as one would either re-introduce the
merge that flattens working 3D or multiply the blit.

The clip is NOT anti-aliased: an AA edge would blend woven and page pixels
in a one-pixel seam, and blending into woven pixels breaks the interlace
lattice — the same reason the draw itself samples NEAREST.

Frame-scoped and SINGLE USE, like the flat list: cleared with the other two
lists at the top of DrawFrame, and drained by phase (B) before its early
returns. A stale flat rect only ghosts; a stale CLIP punches raw page
through a live weave, so "no clip" is the only safe default. Windows only —
the macOS synchronous draw-back still draws the whole window, leaving
frosted elements at their pre-Stage-3 behaviour there.

